James <james.kanze@gmail.com> writes:
> On May 11, 6:27 pm, Jukka Lahtinen <jtfjd...@hotmail.com.invalid>

> > I just don't get it: If you can't deduce the gender from the context, DOES
> > IT REALLY MATTER?
> > What's the real USE for gender-specific pronouns?

> Like any other distinction, they can disambiguate in certain
> cases: "John saw the Mary.  He(she) was eating at the time."

In cases like that, you can always use the name instead of replacing it
with a pronoun. Or use different kinds of structures:
"John saw mary, who was eating" or "John was eating and saw Mary".

I find it irritating that you need expressions like "he or she" when
talking about nobody particular, but a generic anybody who might be male
or female and the pronoun must match his or her gender.

